  'ohelp.org.campaigns.s14h': "Yuno decides who gets what",
  'ohelp.org.campaigns.s14b': "You never pick the audience of a recipe: Yuno computes it and explains it. On each recipe, the “Yuno targets: …” line says who is concerned, and the “eligible right now” counter how many people are at this moment, with the next known send and the event concerned.\nThe source is always your opt-in base: purchases, guest list, form, follows, and imported files (an imported list is fully eligible for New event, Last call and Win-back; never for Welcome). Exclusions are evaluated at send time: already has a spot, bought in the meantime, unsubscribed, unreachable, already written to by an automation within 48 h, already written to for this event by another sender, then the Yuno rules. When several events are due at the same time for one person, they get a single email, the nearest event's.\nWhen your base exceeds what the day's quota allows, the most engaged go first: those who opened or clicked an email in the last 90 days, then those who came in the last 6 months, then this month's sign-ups. The rest go the next day — not a failure, the queue keeps going.\nYuno also suggests turning on what your numbers justify: “Yuno suggests turning on…” (at the top of the Automations page, on the Campaigns page, and a notification once per recipe and per month) lists the recipes that are off but that the last 30 days call for — abandoned checkouts with no follow-up, a close event with a base without tickets, free tables and tickets sold, an event published with no email, scanned events with no thank-you, silent customers, sign-ups with no welcome, an almost full tier — with a “Turn on” button that creates the Yuno template and activates the recipe. Each recipe shows its upcoming sends and its week's report; the customer sheet shows the automatic emails a person received; Monday's recap adds a line “automations: X emails, Y attributed sales”.",
